WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.050
Two of my students both

2
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.910
handed in perfect homework.

3
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.250
In the test, one solved every question.

4
00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.550
The other couldn’t even start.

5
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.840
So what was different?

6
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.760
Today: how to use AI so that

7
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.980
you’re the one who learns.

8
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.340
The difference was who did the thinking.

9
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.360
One worked through the steps

10
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.000
herself, and asked a chatbot to

11
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.200
explain when she got stuck.

12
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.360
The other pasted the questions

13
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.240
in and copied the answers.

14
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.470
Same homework, very different practice.

15
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.850
Your brain learns by trying,

16
00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.330
getting it wrong and fixing it.

17
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.730
That struggle is the learning.

18
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:47.230
If AI does the trying for you, you

19
00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.350
skip the part that teaches.

20
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.390
And in the exam hall, there’s no

21
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.690
chatbot: only what you practised.

22
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.120
Think of a football coach.

23
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.260
A good coach explains, sets

24
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.360
drills and watches you play.

25
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.000
But nobody ever got fitter by

26
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.260
watching the coach run.

27
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.940
Honest use means AI helps you

28
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.940
learn, and you’re open about how.

29
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.880
Asking it to explain, to quiz you or

30
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.840
to check your work is learning.

31
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.580
Asking it to do the work, then

32
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.060
handing that in as yours, is copying,

33
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.240
even if you change a few words.

34
00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.710
Rules differ.

35
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.940
One teacher says no AI for a task;

36
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.740
another allows it for ideas or checking.

37
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.800
Your school’s rules decide, so

38
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.900
ask when you’re not sure.

39
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.920
And many AI tools have a minimum

40
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.100
age, often thirteen, with a parent’s

41
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.200
permission under eighteen.

42
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.060
Quick check!

43
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.710
Which of these is an honest way

44
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.390
to use AI for homework?

45
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.180
“Quiz me on chapter three.”

46
00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.130
You still answer every question yourself,

47
00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.490
and the AI shows you what you missed.

48
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.770
That’s AI as a tutor, not a copy machine.

49
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.090
When you hand work in,

50
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:07.870
say how you used AI.

51
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.060
One line is enough: “I used a

52
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.260
chatbot to explain fractions, then

53
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.420
did the questions myself.”

54
00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.930
Keep your notes and drafts too.

55
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.290
They show your thinking, and tools that

56
00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.350
claim to spot AI writing are often wrong.

57
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.030
In the app, a robot must cross a

58
00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.870
school yard to reach its charger.

59
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.100
Nobody shows it the way.

60
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.840
It learns from its own tries: a reward at

61
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.140
the charger, a penalty in puddles.

62
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.940
Press Practise, and watch

63
00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.120
its trips get shorter.

64
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.160
Your turn.

65
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.700
You used AI to explain a topic,

66
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.480
then did the work yourself.

67
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.880
What should you do when you hand it in?

68
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.830
Say how you used AI: one

69
00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.050
honest line is enough.

70
00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.810
Remember: like the robot, you

71
00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.330
learn from your own tries.

72
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.720
Use AI to explain, to quiz you and to

73
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.320
check your work, never to do it for you.

74
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.580
And your school’s rules decide.

75
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.560
Try it yourself: pick one topic,

76
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.600
and if you’re allowed to use AI,

77
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.160
make it your tutor.

78
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.120
Ask it to quiz you, and answer

79
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.440
every question yourself.

80
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.300
Then ask it to explain your mistakes.

81
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.880
It’s harder than copying, and

82
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.520
that’s exactly why it works.
